{
  "note": "Literals that look like copy and are not. Each is classified so 'this one is not translated' is a decision someone recorded, rather than a pattern that happened not to match. A literal here is never localised; a literal that is copy must be routed through the catalogue instead. A name shown on a screen is never declared here even when it is a brand: it belongs in the catalogue marked shouldTranslate:false, so a translator sees it and knows to leave it. Declaring it internal would hide it from them entirely. Every exclusion is scoped to the file it was decided in. The same words can be a log line in one place and copy on a screen in another, and a global exclusion would silently excuse the second the day someone writes it.",
  "categories": {
    "identifier": "A key, state name or vocabulary token the runtime and the app agree on. Translating it breaks the agreement. It also covers text matched against a response the runtime wrote, where the match is a protocol coupling rather than something a person reads.",
    "sf-symbol": "An SF Symbol name. A translated symbol name is a missing icon.",
    "http": "A header name, method, scheme or media type from a wire protocol.",
    "log": "Text written to this device's own log for an engineer. Never shown to an owner.",
    "test-fixture": "Sample data used to build a preview or a default, not copy on a screen.",
    "brand": "A product or vendor name that is the same in every language.",
    "model-prompt": "An instruction sent to a language model, not shown to anyone. Translating it would change what the model was asked to do.",
    "unroutable-platform": "Copy the platform requires as a compile-time constant, so it cannot be a catalogue lookup. Declared here with the constraint that causes it, rather than left looking like an oversight.",
    "crypto": "A domain separator, PEM marker, OAuth scope or character set that is part of a cryptographic or authorisation construction. Changing the bytes changes what is signed or requested.",
    "shell": "A fragment of the fixed command sent over SSH during assisted install. It is a program's syntax, not a sentence.",
    "match-vocabulary": "An English phrase the app matches against what the owner typed on this device, to decide whether to offer something. Never shown and never sent. A localised build needs its own list, recorded here so that debt is visible rather than implied."
  },
