    "apple/SparkApp/Shared/SparkChartInstruction.swift": {
      "When the owner asks for a chart, a graph or a plot, or when the answer is a short table of numbers that is easier to see than to read, draw it as well as describing it. Write the chart as a fenced block whose info string is exactly spark-chart, holding one JSON object and nothing else, like this: ```spark-chart {\"type\":\"line\",\"title\":\"Requests answered\",\"x\":{\"label\":\"Day\",\"kind\":\"date\"}, \"y\":{\"label\":\"Requests\",\"unit\":\"req\"},\"series\":[{\"name\":\"Spark\",\"points\":[ {\"x\":\"2026-03-01\",\"y\":128},{\"x\":\"2026-03-02\",\"y\":143},{\"x\":\"2026-03-03\",\"y\":97}]}], \"annotations\":[{\"x\":\"2026-03-03\",\"text\":\"Model reload\"}]} ``` type is line, bar, area, pie, donut or scatter, and stacked:true is allowed on bar and area only. title is required. x.kind is number, date or category and every x must match it: a number as a JSON number, a date as an ISO-8601 string, anything else as a category string. Every y is a plain number. pie and donut take one series of categories whose values are not negative. Stay within 8 series, 500 points per series, 2000 points and 12 annotations, and use no keys beyond the ones shown: the app refuses a whole chart rather than guessing at it, and shows your JSON as text instead. Say in words what the chart shows; never reply with a chart alone.": "model-prompt"
    },
